Content-Disposition: form-data; name="explanation"

The result composes two proved steps from the source. The first turns the uniform power-law hypothesis into successive approximants whose logarithmic weights beat an arbitrary history-dependent selector. The second chooses the selector to enforce the $X$ margin and strict $D$-weighted product growth while preserving the approximation data. Its role in the campaign is the normalized approximation-sequence constructor used by later admissible-parameter and determinant estimates.
------p2mBoundary7f3a9c2b
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="file"; filename="Sol_OAI_PiExponent_exists_normalized_successive_approximations.lean"
Content-Type: text/plain

import Lean
import Theorems.Thm_OAI_PiExponent_exists_successive_approximations
import Theorems.Thm_OAI_PiExponent_exists_normalized_selection_of_selector

theorem solution
    (nu X D : ℝ) (hnu : 0 < nu)
    (hbad : ∀ Q : ℕ, ∃ p : ℤ, ∃ q : ℕ,
      Q ≤ q ∧ |Real.pi - (p : ℝ) / q| ≤ (q : ℝ) ^ (-nu)) :
    ∃ p : ℕ → ℤ, ∃ q : ℕ → ℕ, ∃ x : ℕ → ℝ,
      x 0 = 1 ∧
      (∀ n, x (n + 1) = (Nat.ceil (Real.log (q n)) : ℝ)) ∧
      (∀ n, 2 ≤ q n ∧ p n ≠ 0 ∧
        |Real.pi - (p n : ℝ) / q n| ≤ (q n : ℝ) ^ (-nu) ∧
        1 ≤ Nat.ceil (Real.log (q n)) ∧ X < x (n + 1)) ∧
      (∀ i, 1 ≤ x i) ∧
      (∀ i, 0 < i → D * (∏ j ∈ Finset.range i, x j) < x i) := by
  exact OAI.PiExponent.exists_normalized_selection_of_selector nu X D
    (OAI.PiExponent.exists_successive_approximations nu hnu hbad)

